WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.424
The ship's hull trembled as it slipped into the event horizon of the

00:00:04.424 --> 00:00:05.681
young Crude Rift.

00:00:05.681 --> 00:00:09.975
The pilot, Raw, had seen many of these before — but none quite so…

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:11.126
unsettled.

00:00:11.126 --> 00:00:15.615
The swirling mass of spacetime distortion stretched outward in eerie,

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:19.844
convulsing waves, its surface fractured by jagged scars of energy

00:00:19.844 --> 00:00:21.040
displacement.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.530
He'd been to mature sites before — where Crude Matter stabilized into

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.759
lattices, harvested and refined into the fuel that powered entire

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:30.864
fleets.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.000
But this?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.000
This was raw.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Undisciplined.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Dangerous.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.710
He had read the reports — how young Crude Rifts, still forming, still

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.215
pulling themselves together, had torn entire mining vessels apart,

00:00:44.215 --> 00:00:47.082
scattering them across unknown dimensions.

00:00:47.082 --> 00:00:51.860
The hulls would reappear weeks, months, sometimes years later, twisted

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.000
and broken, their crews long lost to the void.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.952
Yet, Crude Matter was the lifeblood of expansion.

00:00:58.952 --> 00:01:01.452
No wars were fought without it.

00:01:01.452 --> 00:01:04.597
No Fabricators operated in its absence.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:10.000
Civilizations rose and fell on the yield of these celestial wounds.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
And so, despite every alarm screaming caution, he pressed forward.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.722
Raw checked his instruments — gravitational distortions were erratic,

00:01:20.722 --> 00:01:25.444
the Stillness effect stronger than recorded at this stage of a Rift's

00:01:25.444 --> 00:01:26.647
life cycle.

00:01:26.647 --> 00:01:28.152
A sign of its potency?

00:01:28.152 --> 00:01:30.000
Or something else entirely?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.079
He activated the scanning array, watching as its pulse rippled across

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:36.504
the event horizon.

00:01:36.504 --> 00:01:40.994
The data feed returned chaotic patterns, unreadable at first.

00:01:40.994 --> 00:01:46.000
Then, a phrase began forming in the noise, distorted yet deliberate:

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.273
"It is not yet born.

00:01:48.273 --> 00:01:51.000
You do not belong here."

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.197
He froze.

00:01:52.197 --> 00:01:55.000
The transmission was not from his ship.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.579
His hands instinctively gripped the throttle, debating whether to

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:00.784
disengage.

00:02:00.784 --> 00:02:05.574
But the Rift pulsed, dragging the ship deeper into its gravity well.

00:02:05.574 --> 00:02:10.576
The young Crude Matter was not stable enough to extract — attempting to

00:02:10.576 --> 00:02:14.239
mine it would likely result in catastrophic failure.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:16.000
But now he was committed.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Something stirred in the distortion ahead.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Not metal.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Not rock.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Not a ship.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Something else.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.102
Raw's mind flashed back to the ghost signal recovered from H-SN21 I, the

00:02:29.102 --> 00:02:33.000
one that still haunted pilots foolish enough to listen.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
"The sin we commit here is too grave to be forgiven."

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
"There is a thing here that suffers no intelligent creature to pass."

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.178
He swallowed hard.

00:02:45.178 --> 00:02:47.156
The similarities were undeniable.

00:02:47.156 --> 00:02:49.013
A force that devours cognition.

00:02:49.013 --> 00:02:51.469
A warning that should have kept him away.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.783
Yet here he was, at the birth of a new wound in the universe, witnessing

00:02:55.783 --> 00:02:58.000
something beyond human comprehension.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.395
The Rift flared, and his ship's comms erupted with overlapping voices —

00:03:03.395 --> 00:03:08.714
his own voice, layered a thousand times over, each iteration distorted

00:03:08.714 --> 00:03:10.000
and broken.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.139
He killed the engines.

00:03:12.139 --> 00:03:17.000
If the Rift wanted him, it would have to take him.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The event horizon convulsed.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
And Raw disappeared.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Weeks later, a long-range probe detected a ship, identical to his,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.091
drifting near the Eschaton system.

00:03:33.091 --> 00:03:37.000
It bore no signs of damage, no crew aboard.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.735
But the logs — uncorrupted and perfectly intact — ended with a single

00:03:42.735 --> 00:03:44.000
message.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.273
"It is not yet born.

00:03:46.273 --> 00:03:49.000
You do not belong here."

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.452
The ship remains classified as a derelict.

00:03:52.452 --> 00:03:55.000
No recovery team has been sent.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
No one wants to know what came back.
